// The implementation lane: strict checkJs over index.js itself. The sibling
// tsconfig.json checks the hand-written index.d.ts against a consumer; nothing
// there ever puts index.js in a program, so JSDoc that doesn't typecheck ships
// silently — found by the first vendoring consumer who type-checks the .js
// (ccr, 2026-08-30). This lane pins that class of bug.
//
// What it does NOT pin, stated plainly: agreement between index.js's JSDoc and
// the hand-written index.d.ts. Both are source (see the d.ts header, gh#2);
// this lane checks the JSDoc's self-consistency only, and drift between the
// two remains a review concern, not a mechanical one.
//
// Run under typescript@5 ONLY (typecheck:js in package.json). The tsc 7
// native preview does not yet narrow on a JSDoc @returns {never} closure
// (fail() in the parser), so it reports strict-null false positives at sites
// TS5 proves guarded. Compiler limitation, not policy — when tsgo learns
// never-narrowing, fold this lane into the both-majors pair.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "strict": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "target": "es2022",
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "files": ["../../index.js"]
}
